Yes. The complete script is essentially a **small local database/editor for matrix objects**, with a filesystem-like interface. The cleanest way to understand it is as several layers working together.

## 1. The overall architecture

The program has five main systems:

```text
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│             MATRIX FILE MANAGER             │
├──────────────────┬──────────────────────────┤
│                  │                          │
│  FOLDER / FILE   │      MATRIX EDITOR       │
│    BROWSER       │                          │
│                  │   Matrix 1               │
│ 📁 experiments   │   [1][2][3]              │
│ 📄 matrix_001   │   [4][5][6]              │
│ 📄 matrix_002   │   [7][8][9]              │
│                  │                          │
│ [New Folder]     │   Matrix 2               │
│ [Delete Folder]  │   [10][20]               │
│                  │   [30][40]               │
└──────────────────┴──────────────────────────┘
```

Internally, it is basically:

```text
Filesystem
    ↓
Matrix files
    ↓
Matrices
    ↓
Rows
    ↓
Variables / cells
```

So the hierarchy is:

```text
Folder
 └── Matrix File
      ├── Matrix 1
      │    ├── Row 1
      │    │    ├── Variable
      │    │    ├── Variable
      │    │    └── Variable
      │    └── Row 2
      │
      ├── Matrix 2
      │
      └── Matrix 3
```

